      discovery: {
        problem:
          "Imagine you have just taken power. You have five tools:\n\n- **Terror** (secret police, prisons, camps)\n- **Propaganda** and a **cult of personality**\n- **Control of young people** (schools, youth movements)\n- **Removing rivals** (banning parties, purges)\n- **Ideology** (a big idea that explains — and justifies — everything)\n\nBefore you read on: which would you need **first**, in your first few months? Which would matter most **in the long run**? Rank them, then compare your ranking with what Hitler, Mao and Pol Pot actually did.",
        idea:
          "Dictators usually remove rivals first: Hitler banned all other parties within six months (July 1933) and had rivals murdered in June 1934. In the long run they relied on a mix of **fear and belief** — terror to punish opponents, propaganda and education to win hearts. Pol Pot relied on terror far more than persuasion, and his purges of his own followers weakened the regime before Vietnam invaded in December 1978.",
      },
      body:
        "Taking power and keeping it are different problems. All three regimes combined **fear** and **belief**, but in different proportions.\n\n| Method | Nazi Germany | Mao's China | Democratic Kampuchea |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Terror | SS (Himmler), Gestapo, concentration camps from 1933 | Campaigns against landlords and 'counter-revolutionaries'; labour camps; Red Guard violence from 1966 | Angkar's purges; S-21 (Tuol Sleng) prison; the killing fields |\n| Propaganda and cult | Goebbels' radio, rallies and films; the Führer cult | Posters, badges, songs and the *Little Red Book*; the cult of Chairman Mao | Radio slogans and meetings; the leaders stayed hidden behind Angkar |\n| Young people | Hitler Youth; a Nazi school curriculum | Red Guards; schools closed in 1966; millions of young people sent to the countryside | Children separated from parents; many trained as soldiers and guards |\n| Removing rivals | Other parties banned (July 1933); Night of the Long Knives (1934) | Anti-Rightist Campaign (1957); purges of top leaders such as Liu Shaoqi | Repeated purges of the Party itself |\n| Ideology and society | Race and nation; private business kept | Class struggle; land collectivised; communes (1958) | 'Year Zero': money, markets, schools and religion abolished |\n\nThe human cost was terrible, and these were crimes. In the Holocaust the Nazis murdered about **6 million Jewish people**, as well as Roma, disabled people and many others. Under Mao, historians estimate that **tens of millions** died, most of them in the famine caused by the Great Leap Forward (1958–62). In Cambodia, around **1.5–2 million people — about a quarter of the population —** died between 1975 and 1979 from execution, starvation, overwork and disease. Behind every number were individual people: parents, children, teachers, farmers, monks.",
